;    1  ! fcalc: the built-in functions.
;    2  !
;    3  ! BUILTINS is the table of every built-in function: its name and how many
;    4  ! arguments it takes, so adding a function is a row here and a CASE in
;    5  ! APPLY_BUILTIN. The evaluator (module FCEVAL) evaluates a call's arguments
;    6  ! into an array and gives it to APPLY_BUILTIN; IF, whose arms are evaluated
;    7  ! only when chosen, is the evaluator's own, and is in the table only so its
;    8  ! name is known.
;    9  !
;   10  ! Every function takes complex values. A real argument in a function's real
;   11  ! domain takes the real path, the Fortran intrinsic (MTHRTL's routine on a
;   12  ! VAX), so sqrt(2) and asin(0.5) are as exact as the machine can make them;
;   13  ! anything else takes the principal branch of the complex function, built
;   14  ! from FCVALUES' guarded arithmetic. As there, nothing traps: each function
;   15  ! checks its argument before the intrinsic sees it (an EXP's argument, a
;   16  ! sine's angle, a pole) and reports OVERFLOW, DOMAIN, or DIVZERO instead.
;   17  !
;   18  ! The trigonometric functions take and give angles in the angle mode
;   19  ! (SET ANGLE): radians, degrees, or grads. In degrees and grads an angle is
;   20  ! first reduced exactly to a turn, and a multiple of a quarter turn has its
;   21  ! exact sine and cosine, so sin(180) is 0 and tan(90) a division by zero.
;   22  !
;   23  ! Some vocabulary of numerical programming used in the comments below:
;   24  !
;   25  ! - Cancellation. Subtracting two nearly equal numbers loses the digits
;   26  !   they share: 1.0000001 - 1.0000000 keeps one significant digit of eight.
;   27  !   A formula that does that for some arguments is rewritten for them (as
;   28  !   LOG1P, ERF's series, and SIN_PI do).
;   29  ! - Principal branch. Functions like the square root and the logarithm
;   30  !   have several values for a complex argument; the principal one is a
;   31  !   fixed, conventional choice, and the line where the choice jumps is the
;   32  !   branch cut (the negative real axis for SQRT and LN).
;   33  ! - Range reduction. A periodic function's argument is brought into one
;   34  !   period before it is evaluated; done in floating point it loses the
;   35  !   argument's low digits, so fcalc does it exactly where it can (in
;   36  !   degrees, by MOD of whole numbers) and refuses angles so large in radians
;   37  !   that no digit of their sine is meaningful.
;   38  ! - Series and continued fractions. A function is often computed by summing
;   39  !   a series until the terms stop mattering (ERF below 1.5), or by a
;   40  !   continued fraction evaluated from a fixed depth back up (ERFC above it);
;   41  !   each converges quickly in its own region, so a function switches
;   42  !   between them at a crossover chosen by testing.
;   43  module fcfuncs
